678 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 327 0 R
/E (Camouflage image datasets \(COD10K and CAMO\) are downsampled to 128x128 pixels and normalized to a [0,1] range for computational efficiency on edge hardware. Advanced augmentation, including Gaussian blur and random contrast jitter, is applied to simulate field conditions. Each static image is transformed into a temporal spike train over $T=16$ timesteps using Poisson Rate Coding, where pixel intensities determine the firing probability of input spikes, ensuring the network captures subtle texture variations.)
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
679 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 328 0 R
/E (Proposed Methodology)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
680 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 328 0 R
/E (Model Architecture)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
681 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 328 0 R
/E (The system utilizes a 3-layer Spiking Convolutional Neural Network \(SCNN\) architecture optimized for the snnTorch framework. The model employs Leaky Integrate-and-Fire \(LIF\) neurons with learnable membrane decay constants beta to adapt to complex camouflage backgrounds. Training is conducted using Backpropagation Through Time \(BPTT\) with an ArcTan \(ATan\) surrogate gradient function, allowing the network to learn weights effectively across discrete spike events. To maximize energy efficiency, the network is designed for 85-90% temporal sparsity, ensuring that power is only consumed when significant visual features trigger a spiking event, allowing for ultra-low power real-time inference on the Jetson Nano.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
682 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (Proposed Methodology)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
683 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (Robot Integration)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
684 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 329 0 R
/E (The trained SNN model is optimized using NVIDIA TensorRT with FP16 quantization and deployed on the Jetson Nano for real-time edge inference. We utilize a high-speed GStreamer pipeline to feed live frames from the CSI camera module directly into GPU memory, minimizing latency. The system is integrated with an L298N motor controller and a custom Python-based navigation script, allowing the ground robot to autonomously signal an alert or modify its path upon detecting a camouflaged threat. The entire unit is powered by a 3-cell Li-Po battery, with power management profiles optimized for extended 2026-standard reconnaissance missions.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
685 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 330 0 R
/E (Proposed Methodology)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
686 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 330 0 R
/T (Performance Benchmarking)
/E (Performance Benchmarking)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
687 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 330 0 R
/E (The system's efficacy is measured using the Mean Average Precision \(mAP@0.5\) metric on a validation subset of the COD10K and CAMO datasets. To demonstrate the technology advantage, the SNN-based system is benchmarked against the state-of-the-art YOLO26-N \(Nano\) model. Key performance indicators include Inference Latency \(ms\) and Energy per Inference \(mJ\), measured using the NVIDIA tegrastats utility. Our primary goal is to validate that the SNN architecture achieves comparable detection accuracy while providing a 50-70% reduction in power consumption, significantly extending the robot's operational battery life in dense camouflage field scenarios.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
